    "chunk_size_bytes": 2001,
    "chunk_text": "The president suggested the MMR vaccine could be \"quite lethal\" if given at once. He compared it to pouring a bottle of soda into a child's body. According to the FDA, a single dose of MMR is 0.5ml.\n\nWhen a reporter asked for evidence of the \"lethal\" claim, Trump said: \"What I've heard is that there are some people that say it is that way.\"\n\nThe agency says \"most people who get MMR vaccine do not have any serious problems with it\", and that getting the vaccine \"is much safer than getting measles, mumps, or rubella\".\n\nAccording to the CDC, there is \"no published scientific evidence that shows any benefit in separating the combination MMR vaccine into three individual shots\".\n\nExperts say that allowing time between jabs means children are more likely to contract diseases in the interim or that they are more likely to miss appointments if there are multiple shots.\n\nDr William Schaffner of the Vanderbilt University School of Medicine in Nashville told the BBC the increased number of visits would be harder for parents and providers to track and would add to the expense without any scientific benefit.\n\nSince taking office in 2025, Secretary of Health and Human Services (HHS) Robert F Kennedy Jr has sought to change and loosen vaccine regulations.\n\nDuring a tense 20-minute interview on CNN last week, Kennedy insisted he had \"never questioned the efficacy of the MMR vaccine\".\n\nHe said the proposed changes were meant to give parents choice, not prohibit access to childhood vaccines.\n\nTrump administration officials have said the order means children in the US would receive much less than the 72 shots recommended under the previous vaccine schedule, a figure that assumes annual flu and Covid-19 doses.\n\nDr Andrew Racine, president of the American Academy of Pediatrics, said Trump's executive order was \"dangerous\" and not based on \"gold-standard science\".\n\n\"Delaying or skipping shots is risky, especially as measles continues to spread and children go back to school,\" Racine said."
