    "md5": "83e7d02735606596e9c6a3984826f8ee",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1699,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, the existence of capital punishment in statutory books, though not implemented, also remains a hindrance when seeking the extradition of Sri Lankan organized criminals hiding in European countries.\n\nMany European countries have abolished the death penalty and consider it a violation of human rights. As a result, they are often hesitant to extradite individuals to countries that have capital punishment due to concerns about the possibility of the suspect facing the death penalty. Some treaties explicitly prohibit extradition if the requested country intends to impose the death penalty, while others require assurances from the requesting country that the death penalty will not be sought or imposed.\n\nSri Lanka has maintained a de facto moratorium on executions for nearly five decades, although courts continue to impose death sentences for serious offences, including murder and drug-related crimes. No execution has been carried out since 1976, with successive governments refraining from implementing death sentences.\n\nThe last judicial execution in Sri Lanka was carried out on June 23, 1976, at Welikada Prison, when convicted criminal Embilipitiye Chandradasa, also known as \u201cHonda Papuwa\u201d, was hanged.\n\nThe issue of resuming executions has been raised by successive governments, most notably during the administration of former President Maithripala Sirisena. In 2018 and 2019, Sirisena pushed for the death penalty to be implemented against convicted drug traffickers, citing the need for tougher measures against the illegal drug trade.\n\nThe move led to the recruitment of executioners and, in June 2019, death warrants were signed for four convicted drug traffickers."
