    "chunk_size_bytes": 1715,
    "chunk_text": "What Sri Lanka\u2019s executioners do when no execution is scheduled\n\nColombo, Aug. 12 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka currently has two members serving as executioners. However, the executioners do not remain on standby solely for capital punishment duties. They are regular employees of the Prisons Department, carrying out other assigned duties and receiving standard government salaries until they are called upon for an execution.\n\nWhen an execution date is officially determined, the executioners report to the prison on the previous day and begin preparations. These include inspecting the gallows and execution equipment and ensuring that all arrangements are in working order.\n\nAccording to the sources, the executioners also assess the condemned prisoner\u2019s physical measurements to determine the required rope length and other technical arrangements. A test is conducted using a weighted sandbag to ensure the equipment is functioning properly before the scheduled execution.\n\nOn the day of an execution, the responsibilities are divided between the two executioners. One assists with the prisoner\u2019s final preparations and accompanies the prisoner to the gallows, while the other remains responsible for operating the execution equipment.\n\nThe position has historically been associated with a hereditary tradition in Sri Lanka, with the role reportedly passed from father to son. However, this practice has since ended, with executioners now recruited through the Prisons Department\u2019s regular employment process.\n\nDespite the nature of their duties, the executioners reported to have remain ordinary employees at the department and perform routine prison-related duties when no execution has been scheduled."
